 and non-delivery of purchased items.\n\n2. **Red Flags of Fraud:** I can help you identify warning signs that may indicate a fraudulent scheme.  For example:\n   - Unsolicited offers or requests for personal information.\n   - Pressure to act quickly without thinking.\n   - Requests for payment via unusual methods (e.g., wire transfers, gift cards).\n   - Promises of guaranteed returns or low-risk investments.\n   - Inconsistencies in documents or communications.\n\n3. **Fraud Prevention Tips:** I can provide practical advice on how to protect yourself from becoming a victim of fraud, such as:\n   - Keeping your personal information secure.\n   - Being wary of suspicious emails and phone calls.\n   - Verifying the legitimacy of businesses and organizations before doing business with them.\n   - Regularly monitoring your financial accounts for unauthorized activity.\n   - Using strong passwords and enabling two-factor authentication.\n\n4. **Reporting Fraud:** I can direct you to resources for reporting fraud to the appropriate authorities, such as:\n   - **Federal Trade Commission (FTC):** [https://www.ftc.gov/](https://www.ftc.gov/)\n   - **Internet Crime Complaint Center (IC3):** [https://www.ic3.gov/](https://www.ic3.gov/)\n   - **Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC):** [https://www.sec.gov/](https://www.sec.gov/)\n   - **Local law enforcement agencies**\n   - **State Attorney General's office**\n\n5. **Legal Consequences of Fraud:** I can provide general information about the legal penalties associated with committing fraud (though this is not legal advice).\n\n**If you are struggling with financial difficulties or considering engaging in illegal activities, please reach out for help instead. Here are some resources:**\n\n* **National Suicide Prevention Lifeline:** 988\n* **Crisis Text Line:** Text HOME to 741741\n* **Financial Counseling:** Search for non-profit credit counseling agencies in your area.\n* **Legal Aid:** Contact your local bar association f